Only the FIRST line of `md --help` is pinned, and deliberately so. The rest of
the help is prose that ArgumentParser re-wraps to the terminal width, and it
changes whenever a flag or a subcommand is added, so pinning it would make this
case fail for reasons that have nothing to do with help being broken.

The first line is worth defending. It carries two facts that must not change
quietly:

  1. `md --help` writes to STDOUT, not stderr. If it went to stderr the pipe
     into `md lines` would see nothing and this case would fail.
  2. It opens with the tool's overview, so a person who types `md --help` is
     told what md is before anything else.

`md` with no arguments at all prints this same help, but the harness cannot
stage that case: run.py rejects an empty `args` file, so there is no way to run
md with an empty argument list.
